er horizontal lines (y = b) for each b, diagonal lines (x+y = c), or sunny lines.\n\nSimilarly for each row b.\n\nWe can try to bound the number of uncovered points after using the non-sunny lines.\n\nAlternatively, we can use a double counting argument: each non-sunny line can cover at most n points (since the maximum number of points in any column, row, or diagonal is n). So total coverage capacity of non-sunny lines is at most (n-2)*n. The total number of points is n(n+1)/2. For n≥5, capacity > total, so coverage is possible in principle. But we need to consider the structure: the lines are parallel to the sides of the triangle, so they cannot cover arbitrary sets.\n\nWe need to show that with only 2 sunny lines, there will be at least one point uncovered.\n\nOne approach: Use a parity argument: The points can be colored in a certain way such that each non-sunny line covers an even number of points of a certain color, while each sunny line covers an odd number, leading to a parity mismatch. But not sure.\n\nAlternatively, consider the sum of coordinates modulo something. For lines parallel to x-axis (y = constant), the sum a+b varies across points. For vertical lines (x = constant), sum varies. For diagonal lines (x+y = constant), sum is constant. So each diagonal line covers points all with same sum. So each diagonal line covers points all with same sum. Horizontal and vertical lines cover points with varying sums.\n\nMaybe we can use a combinatorial argument based on the number of points with a given sum. For each s = 2,...,n+1, the set of points with a+b = s is a diagonal of length s-1 (i.e., there are s-1 points). So the total points are sum_{s=2}^{n+1} (s-1) = n(n+1)/2.\n\nNow, each diagonal line x+y = c (with c integer) can cover at most one of these diagonals (the one with sum c). Actually it covers exactly the points with sum c that are within the triangle (i.e., a,b≥1, a+b ≤ n+1). So a diagonal line x+y = c covers exactly the points with sum c, which are